WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:21.741
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. How microservices relate to cloud architecture.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.447
A monolith on a virtual machine is a cloud deployment of a monolith.

3
00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:43.632
Say it twice, because three different questions hide under the word ‘cloud’, and this episode pulls them apart.

4
00:00:44.612 --> 00:00:52.495
Cloud architecture decides where the software runs, how failure is contained across machines and sites, and which platform services teams may use.

5
00:00:52.875 --> 00:01:01.106
Microservice architecture decides how the application is divided, who owns the data, and how a business action crosses those divisions.

6
00:01:01.486 --> 00:01:12.214
Cloud native is the name for the overlap — where the application is built so it can use the cloud’s elasticity, and microservices are one of the forms that application takes.

7
00:01:12.914 --> 00:01:17.407
The relationship is real, and it is a relationship between two decisions.

8
00:01:18.387 --> 00:01:27.547
NIST fixed the cloud half in September 2011: on-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity, measured service.

9
00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:33.105
Nothing in that definition requires the consumer’s application to be a set of microservices.

10
00:01:33.485 --> 00:01:53.571
The Cloud Native Computing Foundation’s definition, version 1.1, approved on the 26th of February 2024, puts microservices in the other half — on an explicitly non-exhaustive list: containers, service meshes, multi-tenancy, microservices, immutable infrastructure, serverless, declarative APIs.

11
00:01:54.271 --> 00:01:58.113
Microservices are one item on that list.

12
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:04.275
Amazon’s service interfaces became a public cloud in 2006.

13
00:02:04.655 --> 00:02:10.355
Netflix’s 2008 outage pushed a monolith toward AWS and fine-grained services.

14
00:02:10.735 --> 00:02:16.842
Heroku’s Twelve-Factor App, 2011, wrote the contract between an application and a platform.

15
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:23.753
Docker and Kubernetes made the service instance a container the platform could place, restart, and scale.

16
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:29.729
Here is the sentence to keep: the cloud did not invent the service boundary.

17
00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:32.615
It made the boundary payable.

18
00:02:33.655 --> 00:02:39.228
Many processes, many databases, many network calls are expensive on machines you order months ahead.

19
00:02:39.608 --> 00:02:44.194
They are ordinary on a platform that starts a container in seconds and bills for what ran.

20
00:02:45.174 --> 00:02:48.797
Moving to the cloud is a separate step from cutting services.

21
00:02:49.177 --> 00:02:55.295
Rehosting — lift and shift — moves an application onto cloud machines and leaves its architecture in place.

22
00:02:55.675 --> 00:03:02.455
Re-architecting is the later step: decompose, give each service its own store, adopt the collaboration patterns.

23
00:03:03.155 --> 00:03:07.439
Self-contained systems and modular monoliths can also be cloud native.

24
00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.058
They use the platform and keep a larger boundary.

25
00:03:12.378 --> 00:03:16.663
Every microservice decision has a cloud capability that implements it.

26
00:03:17.043 --> 00:03:21.791
Instance per container: Kubernetes and its managed forms, or functions.

27
00:03:22.171 --> 00:03:27.303
Database per service: a managed store of the right type, provisioned for that service.

28
00:03:28.003 --> 00:03:32.298
Messaging and outbox publication: a queue, a topic, a log.

29
00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:36.208
Saga orchestration: a managed state machine.

30
00:03:37.248 --> 00:03:40.719
Circuit breakers and retries: a mesh.

31
00:03:41.759 --> 00:03:45.823
Design for failure: more than one availability zone.

32
00:03:46.863 --> 00:03:50.555
The relationship is the capability, not the brand.

33
00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:54.963
A bounded context does not name a region.

34
00:03:56.003 --> 00:03:59.950
Cloud architecture adds placement and blast radius.

35
00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.158
An availability zone is a data-center-scale failure domain.

36
00:04:06.198 --> 00:04:11.341
A region is a geography, often a data-residency boundary.

37
00:04:12.381 --> 00:04:16.004
An account is an ownership and blast-radius boundary.

38
00:04:17.044 --> 00:04:20.863
Network policy decides which services may call which.

39
00:04:21.903 --> 00:04:26.629
These cuts are orthogonal to the domain cut.

40
00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:31.593
The shared-responsibility line moves with the service model.

41
00:04:32.633 --> 00:04:40.261
And cost is the dark-matter side of the cloud: a design can satisfy the reliability pillar and fail the cost pillar.

42
00:04:40.961 --> 00:04:44.757
That is the microservice premium showing up in the platform.

43
00:04:46.077 --> 00:04:48.991
Team Topologies gives the usual split.

44
00:04:50.031 --> 00:04:55.395
A platform team owns the landing zone, the cluster, the pipeline, and the paved road.

45
00:04:55.775 --> 00:05:02.091
A stream-aligned team owns one service, its repository, its database, and its page in the on-call rotation.

46
00:05:02.791 --> 00:05:07.795
Microservices without that platform team become a crowd of snowflake deployments.

47
00:05:08.175 --> 00:05:14.560
A platform without service boundaries is a cloud hosting a monolith — which is a legitimate architecture.

48
00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.187
An AI claim has to say which layer moved.

49
00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:23.525
Generating a repository, a container file, and a schema is help with the application cut.

50
00:05:23.905 --> 00:05:29.884
Choosing a region, an account boundary, or a network policy is cloud architecture.

51
00:05:30.924 --> 00:05:35.638
Tools that autoscale, restart, and trace act on the platform’s control loop.

52
00:05:36.018 --> 00:05:38.990
Tools that name aggregates act on the domain.

53
00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.468
Keep those effects in separate columns.

54
00:05:43.788 --> 00:05:45.645
Three things to carry away.

55
00:05:46.685 --> 00:05:47.336
One.

56
00:05:48.376 --> 00:05:54.006
Cloud architecture, cloud native, and microservices are three decisions, not one.

57
00:05:55.046 --> 00:05:55.592
Two.

58
00:05:56.632 --> 00:06:01.125
The cloud made the service boundary payable; it did not invent it.

59
00:06:02.165 --> 00:06:02.839
Three.

60
00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:09.579
Zones, regions, accounts, and cost are cuts that domain-driven design does not name.

61
00:06:10.899 --> 00:06:12.513
Next: Kubernetes.

62
00:06:13.553 --> 00:06:19.869
It will keep a well-cut suite, a monolith, and a distributed monolith equally alive — and cannot tell them apart.
